 ## Overview
-Sliced Volume Rendering is a technique that renders volumetric data by dynamically generating $N$ viewport-aligned slice geometries on the fly, which are then rendered using a user-specified GLSL program [S1, unofficial][S2]. It solves the problem of real-time volume visualization with complex lighting and transfer functions by decoupling the geometry generation from the rendering pipeline, allowing for per-pixel lighting calculations (up to 4 lights) and dynamic transfer functions [S1, unofficial]. It differs from standard texture slicing by explicitly generating slice geometry for an Actor rather than relying solely on fixed-function texture mapping, and it differs from [Plane Slicing](845-plane-slicing.md) by automatically aligning slices to the camera viewport to maintain consistent sampling density regardless of viewing angle [S2].
+Sliced Volume Rendering is a technique that renders volumetric data by dynamically generating $N$ viewport-aligned slice geometries on the fly, which are then rendered using a user-specified GLSL program [S1, unofficial][S2]. It solves the problem of real-time volume visualization with complex lighting and transfer functions by decoupling the geometry generation from the rendering pipeline, allowing for per-pixel lighting calculations (up to 4 lights) and dynamic transfer functions [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Texture slicing](25-texture-slicing.md) [G]. It differs from standard texture slicing by explicitly generating slice geometry for an Actor rather than relying solely on fixed-function texture mapping, and it differs from [Plane Slicing](845-plane-slicing.md) by automatically aligning slices to the camera viewport to maintain consistent sampling density regardless of viewing angle [S2].
 
